WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.020
Expenses, the cash book, and paying yourself.

2
00:00:03.370 --> 00:00:07.440
This is the video for Module 3, Expenses and cash records.

3
00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:19.570
An expense is any money the business pays out. There are two very different kinds. Stock purchases are goods you buy to sell again, like bags of rice or bales of fabric.

4
00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:30.100
Running costs are everything else: transport, rent, market levy, nylon bags, phone data, a helper's pay. Keep them in separate columns.

5
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:38.460
Running costs look small next to stock. But if you do not record them, you will think you are making more profit than you are.

6
00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:50.390
For example, Rukayat sells shoes at a Lagos market. Transport costs her about 1,500 naira a day for 6 days. That is 9,000 naira.

7
00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:56.440
The market levy is 300 naira a day, so 1,800 naira.

8
00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:01.640
Two packs of nylon bags cost 2,400 naira.

9
00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:09.420
Data for WhatsApp selling is 1,500 naira, and her Saturday helper gets 3,000 naira.

10
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:21.310
Her running costs come to about 17,700 naira a week. Transport is the biggest, so she looks for a cheaper way, like sharing a keke with a neighbour trader.

11
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:25.480
Keep receipts by month, and screenshot every transfer you pay.

12
00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:36.350
The cash book records every naira in and out, in date order, with a balance after each line. Step one. Count your cash and write it as the opening balance.

13
00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:45.200
Then line by line, add money in and take away money out. Today's closing balance becomes tomorrow's opening balance.

14
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:58.860
At closing, count your cash and compare. For example, Grace in Jos expected 35,000 naira but counted 34,200. She was 800 naira short.

15
00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:06.150
She checked, remembered 800 naira she paid for sachet water for the shop, added the line, and the book balanced.

16
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:16.230
You cannot count bank money with your hands, so keep two sets of columns. Check cash by counting, starting each day with a fixed float of change.

17
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:30.640
Check your bank or wallet balance against your app. Depositing cash at a POS agent is not a sale or an expense, just money changing pockets. But record the agent's charge as an expense.

18
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:42.000
When shop money and house money share one purse, the capital slowly disappears. So think of the business as a separate person who pays you a fixed amount each week.

19
00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.620
For example, Joy sells cooked food.

20
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:49.730
Her weekly sales average about 120,000 naira.

21
00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:58.270
Ingredients cost about 72,000 naira, and gas, transport, packs and levy about 18,000.

22
00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.660
So her weekly profit is about 30,000 naira.

23
00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:12.860
She pays herself 20,000 naira a week and leaves 10,000 in the business to grow and cover slow weeks. If she drew 40,000, her capital would shrink.

24
00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:27.230
Use a separate account for the business, and keep business cash in its own purse. Pay yourself on the same day each week. Write every personal withdrawal, even in an emergency, as a Drawing, not an expense.

25
00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:35.200
And keep cash safe. Do not leave large amounts in the stall overnight, and do not count large sums openly in the market.

26
00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:47.650
To remember. Separate stock from running costs. Keep a running balance and count your cash every day. Keep cash and bank apart. Pay yourself a fixed drawing below your profit.

27
00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:54.680
And each Saturday, bring your totals together on one summary page, then add the weeks into a monthly summary.

28
00:03:55.930 --> 00:04:06.020
Now track one week of expenses, balance your cash book for three days, set your weekly drawing, and do your first monthly summary. Continue with the lessons below.
